This is a device that limit/slow the movement of electron or to set voltage levels within a circuit. Resistors can be fix or variable depending. If a DC source is applied across a voltage, the amount of current flowing  through the resistor can be calculated using Ohm’s law V=I*R. When this happen, the resistor react by dissipating heat to the surrounding, this is known as power dissipation and can be calculated using Power law, P=I*V = R*I^2 (Ohm’s power law) where; R is in ohms, P is the power loss in watts(w), V is voltage(v) and I is the current in ampere(A).
     Unit Prefixes
Resistors comes in value from 1ohms to 1,000,000ohms. For example a 100,000ohms can be simplified by writing 100k ohms, here k=1000.  2,000,000 ohms can be simplified to 2M ohms. Here M=1,000,000.
Conversely, voltage, current and power levels are usually small fractions of a unit in which case it is often easier to use unit prefixes such as milli-m(10^-3), micro(10^-6), nano-n(10^-9) or even pico-p(10^-12). Example, a current of 0.0000594A=5.94*10^-5
Exercise: using an ammeter, you measure a current of 1.0mA through a 4.7k ohms resistor. What voltage must exist across the resistor? How much power does the resistor dissipate?
Solution 
I= 1mA= 1* 10^-3A,     R=4.7k= 4.7*10^3 ohms
Using Ohms law, V=IR
V=(1*10^-3) *(4.7*10^3)
V=4.7V
Using ohms power law
P=I^2 *R
P= (1*10^-3)^2 *(4.7*10^3)
P =4.7*10^-3W   =4.7mW


