DIODES
This is a two lead semiconductor that act like a one-way gate to electric current flow. They are device formed from the sandwiching of two semiconductors i.e the N-type semiconductors and the p-type semiconductors. The cathode normally contain the N-type while the anode contain the P-type. If the anode is made more positive than the cathode when a voltage supply is connected, current will flow in the forward direction; this is know as a forward bias. And when the anode is made more negative than the cathode, the resulting arrangement is called a reverse bias, current would not flow because the diode act to block the current.
     The P-type semiconductor carries a hole in it structure while the N-type semiconductor carries electron in it structure because the are having extra electron due the covalent bonding of silicon and phosphorus. The one-way gate is made possible because of the interaction between the charge carrying side, when a voltage is applied across it, the electrons will get attracted by the holes therefore causing current to follow in one direction. Both the N-type semiconductors and the P-type semiconductors conduct electricity; one does it with electron while the other does it with holes. When a diode is connected to a voltage source; the positive side of the battery connected to the anode while the negative side of the battery to the cathode(N-type side), current flows through the battery forcing the holes to pull the electrons towards the centre of the diode. The electron and holes combines allowing current to pass through the diode – this is know as a forward bias.
        A diode one-way gate does not work all the time because it requires certain minimal amount of voltage current to follow through the diode. For silicon diode, the applied voltage would be 0.6v or greater while for germanium it is 0.2v.

  You can easily tell which side is the anode or cathode by looking the direction of the arrow or but looking at the distance of the colour band,  the arrow is pointing towards the right --- from the anode to the cathode. But for a physical diodes, the side closer to the black band is the cathode.
