TRANSISTORS:
These are semiconductor device that can electrically control switches or amplify control. Transistors are used in almost every electrical circuit you can imagine. For example, in switches circuit, amplifiers circuit, oscillator circuit, current-source circuit, power circuit, digital logic integrated circuit, voltage regulator circuit and also any circuit that use small control signal.
     They come in various design and unique control and current-flow features. Most comes with a variable current-control features. Some transistors are normally OFF until a voltage is applied to the base or gate where as other are normally ON until voltage is applied to the base or gate. Some transistor require small voltage and small current applied to their control lead where as other require just voltage.
     Their are two major families of transistors; Bipolar transistors and the field effect transistors(FETs). The major difference is that bipolar transistor requires a biasing input(or output) current at their control leads, where as FETs requires only a voltage----practically  no current. Because of the nature of FETs, they draw little to no current and so they have high impedance. FETs are more popular that bipolar in circuit today. Aside drawing little current, they are cheap to manufacture (doesn’t need much silicon) and they can be extremely small.
     Bipolar transistors are three terminal device that act like an electrical control switch or an amplifier. They are used in either NPN or PNP configuration. The NPN uses small current  and positive voltage at the base to control larger collector—emitter current. Conversely, the PNP uses small output current and negative voltage to control large emitter-collector current. The fundament formula to determine the behaviour of bipolar; Ic =H*Ib =B*Ib
Ie= Ic+Ib,   Where; Ie is the emitter current, Ic is the collector current and Ib is the base current. B(H) is the current gain. 
For most cases Ie=Ic 
