RELAY
These are electrical actuated switches. The basic kinds of relays are mechanical relays, reed relays, and solid-state relays. For a typical mechanical relays, a current sent through a coil magnet act to pull a flexible, spring—loaded conductive plate from one switch contact to another. Reed relays consist of a pair of reeds (thin, flexible metal strips) that spring together whenever a current is sent through an encapsulating wire coil. Solid-state relay is a device that can be used to switch states by applying external voltage across N-type and P-type semiconductive junction. Some limitation of solid-state and reed relay include; limited switching arrangement and tendency to become damaged by surge in power. Overall, the solid-state and the reed relays are the fastest (0.2ms-10s).
     To make a relay change states, the voltage across the leads of its magnetic coil should be at least withing + or – 25 percent of the relay’s specified controlled voltage rating. Too much voltage may damage the magnetic coil, whereas to little may cause the relay to act erratically( flipping between back and front).
     The coil of a relay act like an inductor. Inductors do not like sudden changes in current, if the current through the coil is suddenly interrupted by let say, a switch is opened, the coil will respond by producing  a large surge of voltage across its leads, thereby causing a large surge of current through it. The trick in getting rid of spikes is to use what are called Transient suppressors. Diodes can be placed in reverse bias across a relay’s coil to eliminate voltage spikes by going into conduction before a large voltage can be form. When it comes to dealing with an AC-actuated relays, using a diode to eliminate voltage spike will not work—the diode will conduct on alternate half cycles. Instead a RC series network is placed across the coil can be used. The capacitor absorb excessive charge and the resistor help control the discharge.

